CARD 1 · DEFINITION
</div>

<h2>What Is State Responsibility?</h2>

<div class="definition">

State responsibility is the principle of international law that a state is legally responsible when conduct attributable to it breaches an applicable international obligation.

</div>

<p>
A state may become responsible through an act, an omission, the conduct of its organs or officials, or other conduct that international law attributes to the state.
</p>

<p>
State responsibility is distinct from individual criminal responsibility, which concerns the personal responsibility of individuals.
</p>

<div class="formula">

Attribution + Breach of an International Obligation = Internationally Wrongful Act

</div>

<p>
The central question is:
<strong>When is a state legally responsible for violating international law?</strong>
</p>

</div>


<!-- CARD 2 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 2 · INTERNATIONALLY WRONGFUL ACT
</div>

<h2>The Internationally Wrongful Act</h2>

<p>
The central concept in state responsibility is the <strong>internationally wrongful act</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Under the International Law Commission's Articles on Responsibility of States for Internationally Wrongful Acts, an internationally wrongful act generally exists when:
</p>

<ol>

<li>The conduct is attributable to the state.</li>

<li>The conduct constitutes a breach of an international obligation of that state.</li>

</ol>

<div class="formula">

Conduct Attributable to State + Breach = Internationally Wrongful Act

</div>

<p>
The conduct may consist of either an act or an omission.
</p>

</div>


<!-- CARD 3 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 3 · ACT OR OMISSION
</div>

<h2>Acts and Omissions</h2>

<p>
State responsibility can arise from both positive conduct and failures to act.
</p>

<h3>Act</h3>

<p>
A state may incur responsibility because it does something prohibited by international law.
</p>

<p>
For example, a state unlawfully uses force against another state.
</p>

<h3>Omission</h3>

<p>
A state may also incur responsibility because it fails to do something required by an international obligation.
</p>

<p>
For example, a state may fail to take required protective measures under an applicable human-rights obligation.
</p>

<div class="definition">

State responsibility can arise from action or failure to act.

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 4 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 4 · ATTRIBUTION</div>

<h2>Attribution</h2>

<p>
Attribution determines whether particular conduct is legally considered conduct of the state.
</p>

<p>
Because states act through people and institutions, international law establishes rules for deciding when conduct is attributable to the state.
</p>

<p>
Conduct may be attributable where it is performed by:
</p>

<ul>

<li>State organs.</li>

<li>Government officials.</li>

<li>Entities exercising governmental authority.</li>

<li>Certain persons or groups acting under state direction or control.</li>

<li>Other persons whose conduct international law specifically attributes to the state.</li>

</ul>

<div class="definition">

Attribution asks: <strong>Can this conduct legally be treated as conduct of the state?</strong>

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 5 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 5 · STATE ORGANS
</div>

<h2>Conduct of State Organs</h2>

<p>
The conduct of a state organ is generally attributable to the state.
</p>

<p>
State organs can include:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Legislatures.</li>

<li>Courts.</li>

<li>Government ministries.</li>

<li>Police.</li>

<li>Military forces.</li>

<li>Administrative agencies.</li>

</ul>

<p>
The constitutional structure of the state does not normally prevent an institution from being considered a state organ for purposes of international responsibility.
</p>

<div class="formula">

State Organ → Conduct → Attribution to the State

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 6 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 6 · OFFICIAL CONDUCT
</div>

<h2>Legislative, Executive & Judicial Conduct</h2>

<h3>Legislative Conduct</h3>

<p>
A state may incur responsibility if its legislature adopts legislation that breaches an international obligation.
</p>

<h3>Executive Conduct</h3>

<p>
The actions of ministers, officials, police, military personnel, and other executive authorities can be attributable to the state.
</p>

<h3>Judicial Conduct</h3>

<p>
Courts are also state organs. A state can therefore be responsible for judicial conduct that breaches an applicable international obligation.
</p>

<div class="definition">

A state generally cannot avoid international responsibility simply by arguing that the violation came from a different branch of government.

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 7 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 7 · EXCESS OF AUTHORITY & PRIVATE ACTORS
</div>

<h2>Officials Acting Beyond Authority</h2>

<p>
A state may remain responsible when one of its officials exceeds their authority or acts contrary to instructions.
</p>

<p>
For example, a police officer may be prohibited by domestic law from using unlawful force but nevertheless act in an official capacity.
</p>

<p>
The state may still incur international responsibility for the conduct.
</p>

<h3>Private Individuals</h3>

<p>
A state is not automatically responsible for every act committed by a private person.
</p>

<p>
However, international law recognizes circumstances in which private conduct may become attributable to the state.
</p>

<div class="formula">

Domestic Disobedience ≠ Automatic Escape from International Responsibility

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 8 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 8 · GOVERNMENTAL AUTHORITY & CONTROL
</div>

<h2>Private Persons, Governmental Authority & Control</h2>

<h3>Persons Exercising Governmental Authority</h3>

<p>
A state may entrust private entities or individuals with elements of governmental authority.
</p>

<p>
Where such persons act in that governmental capacity, their conduct may be attributable to the state.
</p>

<h3>Direction and Control</h3>

<p>
Conduct of private persons or groups may, in certain circumstances, be attributable to a state where the state directed or controlled the conduct.
</p>

<p>
This issue can be particularly important in situations involving:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Armed groups.</li>

<li>Military operations.</li>

<li>Proxy forces.</li>

<li>Other organized groups.</li>

</ul>

<div class="definition">

Private conduct is not automatically state conduct. Attribution depends on the applicable international-law rules and circumstances.

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 9 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 9 · BREACH OF AN OBLIGATION
</div>

<h2>Breach of an International Obligation</h2>

<p>
Attribution alone does not create state responsibility.
</p>

<p>
The conduct must also breach an international obligation binding on the state.
</p>

<p>
International obligations can arise from:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Treaties.</li>

<li>Customary international law.</li>

<li>General principles of law.</li>

<li>Other recognized sources of international law.</li>

</ul>

<h3>Timing</h3>

<p>
The relevant obligation must generally be binding on the state at the time of the conduct.
</p>

<div class="formula">

Applicable International Obligation + Breach → Possible State Responsibility

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 10 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 10 · CONTINUING & COMPOSITE ACTS
</div>

<h2>Continuing and Composite Breaches</h2>

<h3>Continuing Breach</h3>

<p>
Some wrongful acts continue over a period of time.
</p>

<p>
For example, if an unlawful situation remains in existence, the breach may continue for as long as that situation persists.
</p>

<h3>Composite Acts</h3>

<p>
Some international wrongs result from a series of acts or omissions rather than one isolated event.
</p>

<p>
Where international law defines the violation as arising from a series of actions, responsibility may be assessed in relation to the conduct as a whole.
</p>

<div class="definition">

A wrongful act may consist of one event, a continuing situation, or a series of conduct, depending on the applicable international obligation.

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 11 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 11 · HUMAN RIGHTS & USE OF FORCE
</div>

<h2>State Responsibility in Practice</h2>

<h3>Human Rights</h3>

<p>
State responsibility is especially important in international human-rights law.
</p>

<p>
Responsibility may arise where authorities violate human rights or where the state fails to comply with applicable protective or investigative obligations.
</p>

<h3>Use of Force</h3>

<p>
International law places important restrictions on the use of force by states.
</p>

<p>
An unlawful use of force may breach an international obligation and give rise to state responsibility, particularly under the UN Charter framework.
</p>

<h3>Territorial Integrity</h3>

<p>
Violations of another state's territorial integrity may also engage state responsibility where prohibited by international law.
</p>

</div>


<!-- CARD 12 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 12 · CIRCUMSTANCES PRECLUDING WRONGFULNESS
</div>

<h2>When Wrongfulness May Be Precluded</h2>

<p>
International law recognizes limited circumstances in which conduct that would otherwise breach an obligation may not be considered wrongful.
</p>

<p>
The International Law Commission's Articles include:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Consent.</li>

<li>Self-defence.</li>

<li>Countermeasures.</li>

<li>Force majeure.</li>

<li>Distress.</li>

<li>Necessity.</li>

</ul>

<div class="definition">

These doctrines are narrow and subject to strict conditions. They are not general excuses for unlawful conduct.

</div>

<h3>Important Point</h3>

<p>
Each circumstance must be examined separately under the applicable rules of international law.
</p>

</div>


<!-- CARD 13 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 13 · CONSEQUENCES & REPARATION
</div>

<h2>What Happens After Responsibility?</h2>

<p>
When a state is responsible for an internationally wrongful act, international law generally requires the state to:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Cease the wrongful conduct where it is continuing.</li>

<li>Make appropriate reparation.</li>

<li>Provide assurances or guarantees of non-repetition where appropriate.</li>

</ul>

<h3>Forms of Reparation</h3>

<table class="comparison">

<tr>
<th>Form</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>

<tr>
<td><strong>Restitution</strong></td>
<td>Restore the situation that existed before the wrongful act where possible and appropriate.</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td><strong>Compensation</strong></td>
<td>Provide monetary reparation for financially assessable damage.</td>
</tr>

<tr>
<td><strong>Satisfaction</strong></td>
<td>Provide appropriate non-monetary reparation, such as acknowledgment or an expression of regret.</td>
</tr>

</table>

</div>


<!-- CARD 14 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 14 · ERGA OMNES & INTERNATIONAL CLAIMS
</div>

<h2>Invoking State Responsibility</h2>

<h3>Injured State</h3>

<p>
The state directly affected by an internationally wrongful act may generally be considered the injured state.
</p>

<p>
It may have legal rights to invoke the responsibility of the responsible state, subject to applicable international law.
</p>

<h3>Diplomatic Protection</h3>

<p>
A state may sometimes exercise diplomatic protection on behalf of one of its nationals who has suffered an internationally wrongful act by another state.
</p>

<h3>Erga Omnes Obligations</h3>

<p>
Some international obligations are owed to the international community as a whole.
</p>

<p>
These are commonly described as <strong>obligations erga omnes</strong>.
</p>

<div class="definition">

Because of their collective importance, breaches of erga omnes obligations may raise legal interests for states beyond the state directly injured.

</div>

</div>


<!-- CARD 15 -->

<div class="note-card">

<div class="card-number">
CARD 15 · KEY TAKEAWAY
</div>

<h2>Why State Responsibility Matters</h2>

<p>
State responsibility connects international obligations with legal accountability and remedies.
</p>

<p>
It helps:
</p>

<ul>

<li>Enforce international obligations.</li>

<li>Hold states accountable.</li>

<li>Protect treaty commitments.</li>

<li>Protect customary international law.</li>

<li>Provide legal consequences for wrongful conduct.</li>

<li>Support peaceful dispute settlement.</li>

<li>Provide reparation to injured parties.</li>

<li>Strengthen human-rights protection.</li>

<li>Reinforce the international rule of law.</li>

</ul>

<div class="formula">

Attribution → Breach → Internationally Wrongful Act → Responsibility → Consequences

</div>

<h3>Simple State Responsibility Test</h3>

<ol>

<li><strong>Identify the obligation:</strong> What international rule binds the state?</li>

<li><strong>Identify the conduct:</strong> What act or omission occurred?</li>

<li><strong>Determine attribution:</strong> Is the conduct legally attributable to the state?</li>

<li><strong>Determine breach:</strong> Does the conduct violate the international obligation?</li>

<li><strong>Consider wrongfulness:</strong> Does a circumstance such as valid self-defence or necessity apply?</li>

<li><strong>Determine consequences:</strong> What cessation or reparation is required?</li>

</ol>

<div class="definition">

<strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> State responsibility means international accountability of a state for wrongful conduct attributable to it.

</div>

